    "pros": [
      "Formulated for both concrete and wood floors, inside and out",
      "Benjamin Moore's full color system available",
      "Satin and low-luster sheens hide wear well",
      "Smooth finish is easy to sweep and mop",
      "Good mildew resistance for exterior use"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not intended for garage floors with vehicle traffic",
      "Priced at the premium end of latex floor paints"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A premium-looking finish for patios, porches, sunrooms and basement living spaces that see feet, not tires.",
    "description": "Benjamin Moore's Floor and Patio enamel is the choice when appearance matters as much as toughness. It is an acrylic latex floor enamel made for concrete and wood, interior or exterior, and it can be tinted to almost any color in the Benjamin Moore system, which is a real advantage when you are matching trim, siding or a basement color scheme. The finish lays down smoothly and has a refined, low-luster look that hides footprints and minor scuffs better than glossy garage paints. It is not built for vehicle traffic, and Benjamin Moore does not position it as a garage product, so keep it to patios, porches, sunrooms, mudrooms and finished basement areas. Expect to pay more per gallon than big-box floor paint, partly for the color matching and partly for the brand's dealer network, where staff can advise on primers for previously painted or bare concrete. Owners tend to report good color retention outdoors when the slab was clean and dry before painting."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Concrete Floor Paint",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-concrete-floor-paint/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best paint for a concrete floor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes, an epoxy-modified acrylic floor paint such as BEHR Premium 1-Part Epoxy is the best all-round choice, because it is tougher than plain latex but still rolls on like paint. For patios and living spaces, a premium acrylic enamel looks better. For stairs and wet areas, choose a slip-resistant coating. Daily vehicle parking is better served by a two-part system."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need to etch concrete before painting it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, yes. Power-troweled garage slabs and smooth basement floors are too dense for paint to grip, and etching opens the surface so the paint can bond. Older, rough or previously ground slabs may already have enough profile. A simple guide is texture: if the floor feels smoother than medium sandpaper, etch or grind it before painting."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I paint concrete that already has paint on it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, as long as the old paint is firmly bonded and compatible. Clean it, scuff-sand it to remove the gloss, and test adhesion with duct tape in several places. If old paint lifts with the tape, scrape or grind those areas back to bare concrete. Avoid putting latex over a glossy oil-based floor enamel without a bonding primer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does concrete floor paint take to dry?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most one-part floor paints are dry to the touch in one to four hours and ready for a second coat in about four to twelve hours, depending on the product and weather. Light foot traffic is generally fine after a day, furniture after a few days, and vehicles after roughly a week. Cool, humid conditions lengthen every stage."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is concrete floor paint slippery when wet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Smooth satin and gloss floor paints can be slippery when wet, especially outdoors or on stairs. Add an anti-slip additive to the final coat or choose a textured product such as INSL-X Sure Step or KILZ Over Armor for steps, ramps, pool decks and entries. Indoors, satin finishes are usually safe for dry floors but use mats near doors."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does painted concrete last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Expect roughly three to five years in a garage with car traffic, five to seven years in a basement or utility space, and two to four years on an exposed patio, assuming good prep. Floors that were properly cleaned, etched and moisture-tested last longest. Rolling a maintenance coat before the paint wears through extends the life considerably."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is paint or epoxy better for a garage floor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A two-part epoxy is more durable, thicker and much better at resisting hot tire pickup, chemicals and abrasion. One-part floor paint is cheaper, easier and more forgiving, and perfectly good for garages used mainly for storage, hobbies or occasional parking. If you park daily and want a floor that lasts many years, a two-part coating is worth the extra cost."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Paint & Floor Coatings",
    tags: [
      "best concrete floor paint",
      "concrete floor paint",
      "one part epoxy floor paint",
      "garage floor paint",
      "patio floor paint",
      "how to paint concrete"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          The 6 Best Concrete Floor Paints, Ranked by Room
        </h1>
        <QuickAnswer products={PRODUCTS} />
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          Single-component paints you can open, stir and roll the same afternoon. Here is which one fits your slab, and the prep that decides whether it lasts.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          We judge every product on its spec sheet, its label and what owners report over time, not on the marketing. No paid placements, and commission never sets a ranking. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">11 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>Top Picks</span></a>
              <a href="#paint-or-coating" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>Paint or Coating?</span></a>
              <a href="#paint-types" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>Floor Paint Types</span></a>
              <a href="#will-it-stick" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>Will Paint Stick?</span></a>
              <a href="#prep" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>Prep Steps</span></a>
              <a href="#rolling" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Rolling It Out</span></a>
              <a href="#coverage-cost" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Coverage and Cost</span></a>
              <a href="#room-by-room" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Room by Room</span></a>
              <a href="#upkeep" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Lifespan and Upkeep</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Concrete floor paint is the cheapest way to make a gray slab look finished, and also the easiest floor project to get wrong. The paint is rarely the problem. A sealed slab, a damp slab, or a floor that gets driven on three days after painting causes almost every failure people blame on the can. Pick the right one-part product for the room, do the prep the label asks for, and a painted floor gives you three to five good years before it needs a refresh coat.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Concrete Floor Paints, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These picks are single-component paints and coatings sold at mainstream retailers. We ranked them on what the manufacturers publish — resin type, coverage, recoat and cure windows, recommended surfaces — together with warranty terms and recurring patterns in owner feedback. We did not run our own lab or wear tests on these products, and we say so wherever a claim rests on a label rather than on the floor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="paint-or-coating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Floor Paint or a Floor Coating: Decide This First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Everything on this page is a one-part product. You open the can, stir it, and roll it. That single fact separates floor paint from the two-part epoxies, polyaspartics and polyurea systems covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-floor-coating/">best concrete floor coatings</a>. Those systems cure by chemical reaction between a resin and a hardener, build a much thicker film, and last much longer under car tires. They also cost three to ten times as much per square foot and punish mistakes in prep and timing.
          </p>

          <p>
            One-part paint dries as water or solvent evaporates and the binder coalesces into a film. That film is thinner and softer than a reacted epoxy, usually somewhere around 2 to 4 mils dry per coat, compared with 10 mils or more for a two-part system and far more for a full broadcast floor. The thinness is why paint is forgiving to apply and cheap to touch up. It is also why paint wears through in traffic lanes and why it is prone to lifting where hot tires sit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The decision is mostly about wheels. If a car, truck or loaded cart parks on the floor every day, one-part paint will work for a while but a two-part system is the better long-term buy. If the floor sees feet, furniture, a workbench and the occasional lawn mower, paint is the right tool. A basement rec room, a laundry area, a covered patio, a porch slab or a garage used mainly for storage all fall comfortably in paint territory. An outdoor driveway is a different job with its own products, covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-driveway-paint-reviews/">best driveway paints</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE QUICK RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Feet and furniture: paint. Daily tires: two-part coating.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">One-part floor paint can survive a parked car if it is fully cured and the slab was properly etched, but it is the first place paint fails. If you park on the floor every night and want five-plus years, budget for a two-part kit instead.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            There is a middle ground worth knowing. Epoxy-modified acrylics like the BEHR and Rust-Oleum one-part products at the top of our list blend a small amount of epoxy resin into an acrylic paint. They are harder and more chemical-resistant than plain latex floor paint, but they are still one-part paints. The word epoxy on the label does not make them a garage coating system, and expecting them to behave like one is the single most common source of disappointed reviews.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="paint-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Four Kinds of Concrete Floor Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Retail shelves sort floor paint by brand, not by chemistry, which makes it hard to compare. Underneath the marketing there are four practical families, and knowing which one you are holding tells you more than the front label does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acrylic latex floor enamel</h3>

          <p>
            Water-based acrylic made with harder resins than wall paint. It is low odor, cleans up with water, tints to almost any color, and stays slightly flexible, which helps it ride out temperature swings on patios and porches. It is the right choice for basements, sunrooms and outdoor slabs with foot traffic. It is the softest of the four, so it scuffs under furniture legs and wears through quickest in doorways.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Epoxy-modified acrylic</h3>

          <p>
            Still water-based and still one-part, but with epoxy resin blended in for a harder, more stain-resistant film. This is the most popular garage paint category because it bridges the gap between latex and a real epoxy kit. It costs a little more than plain acrylic and generally carries longer cure times before vehicles are allowed on it. For most homeowners painting a garage or utility floor, this is the sweet spot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Alkyd and oil-modified enamel</h3>

          <p>
            Solvent-based floor enamels dry harder and level beautifully, and they were the standard for porch and shop floors for decades. They have largely retreated because of VOC rules, strong odor, slow dry times and a tendency to yellow indoors. Alkyd also reacts badly with fresh or alkaline concrete, which can break down the binder. Unless you are matching an existing oil-based floor, most people are better served by a modern acrylic.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Textured and anti-slip coatings</h3>

          <p>
            Products like KILZ Over Armor and INSL-X Sure Step carry grit or a thick resurfacing body in the can. They trade a smooth, easy-clean surface for traction and the ability to hide a rough slab. Use them where people may slip or where the concrete is too worn for a thin paint to look good, and keep smooth paint everywhere else.
          </p>

