        <div id="will-it-stick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Three Tests That Tell You Whether Paint Will Stick
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paint fails on concrete for three reasons: something on the surface stops it bonding, moisture coming up through the slab pushes it off, or the surface is too smooth for the paint to grip. Each has a simple test you can run in an afternoon with tools you already own, and skipping them is the reason so many painted floors peel within a year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The water-drop test for sealers</h3>

          <p>
            Sprinkle a few tablespoons of water on several spots, including the middle of the floor and near walls. If the water darkens the concrete and soaks in within a minute or so, the surface is open and ready. If it beads up or sits on top, there is a sealer, curing compound, wax or old coating in the way. Paint will stick to that layer, not to the concrete, and come off with it. The slab needs to be stripped or mechanically ground before painting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The plastic-sheet test for moisture</h3>

          <p>
            Tape an 18 by 18 inch square of clear plastic to the floor, sealing all four edges, and leave it at least 16 hours, which is what ASTM D4263 specifies; a full day is better. If droplets form under the plastic or the concrete beneath it looks darker than the surrounding slab, moisture is moving up through the floor. Paint will blister and peel. Fix the moisture first, using our guide to <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">fixing a damp basement</a> if the slab is below grade, or move to a moisture-tolerant system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The fingernail and sandpaper test for texture</h3>

          <p>
            Run your hand across the floor. A good surface for paint feels like 120 to 150 grit sandpaper. If it feels as smooth as glass, typical of power-troweled garage slabs, the paint has nothing to grab and needs an acid etch or light grinding first. On an old painted floor, press a strip of duct tape firmly onto a few spots and rip it off. Any paint that comes up with the tape has to be removed before the new coat goes on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Prep: The Steps the Label Assumes You Did
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every floor paint label opens with a line that says the surface must be clean, dry, sound and free of contaminants. That sentence carries most of the weight. The paint can only perform as well as the surface it is bonded to, and most of the work on a painted floor happens before the can is opened. Plan a full day for prep on a two-car garage and half a day on a basement room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by clearing the room completely and scraping off anything stuck to the floor: drywall mud, paint drips, adhesive. Sweep, then degrease. Oil and transmission fluid are the enemy of adhesion, and a mop-and-bucket clean will not remove them. Scrub stained areas with a concrete degreaser and a stiff brush, rinse, and repeat until water no longer beads on the spot. For old, deep stains our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> covers poultices and stronger cleaners.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next comes profiling. On most residential slabs that means an acid etch: a citric acid etch sold in garage-floor kits, or diluted muriatic acid for harder, smoother concrete. Wet the floor, apply the etch evenly, scrub it in, and watch for gentle fizzing, which tells you it is working. Rinse thoroughly, at least twice, and let the slab dry completely. Very smooth or very hard floors respond better to a rented diamond grinder, which also removes thin old coatings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fill cracks and pits after profiling and before painting, using a concrete patch or crack filler that the manufacturer says can be painted. Let it cure fully. Then vacuum the entire floor with a shop vacuum; dust left after etching or grinding is a hidden bond-breaker. Finally, wait for the slab to dry. After a wash or etch that is often 24 to 48 hours, longer in a cool, humid basement.
          </p>

