        <div id="rolling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Rolling It Out: Technique, Coats and Timing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check the forecast before you check the paint aisle. Most floor paints want air and slab temperatures roughly between 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit, with the slab at least 5 degrees above the dew point so condensation does not form under the wet film. Painting a garage in the morning sun, when the slab is still cool and the air is warming fast, invites bubbles and poor bonding. Late morning on a mild, dry day is the sweet spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the label calls for a primer on bare concrete, use the recommended one. Some epoxy-modified paints are self-priming when the first coat is thinned or applied lightly; others call for a dedicated concrete primer, especially on porous or chalky slabs. Then cut in the edges with a brush along walls and around posts, working a few feet at a time so the cut line stays wet when the roller reaches it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Roll with a 3/8 to 1/2 inch nap roller on a pole, depending on the label and the slab texture. Work in sections about 4 by 6 feet, laying the paint on in a W pattern and then smoothing it with parallel strokes back into the wet edge. Keep the coat thin. A thick first coat feels productive but traps solvent, stays soft longer, and is more likely to peel. Two thin coats always beat one heavy coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Respect the recoat window printed on the can. Too early, and the second coat softens the first; too late on some epoxy-modified paints, and the first coat has cured enough that the second will not bond without scuffing. After the final coat, light foot traffic is usually allowed in about a day, furniture in a few days, and vehicles typically after about a week. Cool weather stretches all of those numbers.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE CURE CLOCK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Dry to the touch is not the same as cured.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A floor that feels dry in four hours is still soft underneath. Tire plasticizers bond to under-cured paint and peel it off when the car backs out. Wait the full vehicle cure on the label, and longer in cool weather, before parking on it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Coverage and Cost: How Much Paint You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Smooth floor paints typically list spread rates somewhere around 300 to 400 square feet per gallon per coat on smooth, sealed-pore concrete, and noticeably less on porous or freshly etched slabs, where the first coat soaks in. Textured coatings cover far less, often a quarter to a third of that area, because they go on thick. Always use the rate printed on the specific product label rather than a generic figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            A standard two-car garage is around 400 to 500 square feet. With two coats of a smooth one-part paint, that is about 800 to 1,000 square feet of coverage, which works out to roughly 3 gallons, plus a margin for a porous first coat. A single-car garage needs about half that. A 12 by 16 foot basement room at 192 square feet will usually take one gallon for two coats, with a little left for touch-ups.
          </p>

