        <div id="room-by-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Paint for Which Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Garage.</strong> Use an epoxy-modified acrylic like the BEHR or Rust-Oleum one-part products, etch or grind first, and wait the full vehicle cure. If you work on cars, use jack stands on plywood pads so the stands do not punch through the film. If you want a garage floor that genuinely shrugs off hot tires, skip to our ranking of the <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">best garage floor epoxy</a> and plan for a weekend project instead of an afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Basement.</strong> Moisture testing matters more here than anywhere else, because below-grade slabs breathe water vapor. Use a breathable acrylic or epoxy-modified paint labeled for basement floors, avoid alkyds, and run a dehumidifier during cure. Our separate guide to the <a href="/best-basement-floor-paint/">best basement floor paint</a> goes deeper into damp-slab products and what to do when the plastic-sheet test fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Patios and porch slabs.</strong> Outdoor concrete needs UV-stable color, some flexibility for freeze and thaw, and traction when wet. An acrylic enamel such as Benjamin Moore Floor and Patio handles that well, and adding an anti-slip additive or using a textured coating on steps is cheap insurance. For wood porches and mixed wood-and-concrete entries, see our <a href="/best-porch-paint/">best porch paint</a> picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Workshops and utility rooms.</strong> Boots, carts and dropped tools favor a harder, faster-drying acrylic like Tread-Plex. Consider painting walkway lines or a contrasting border with a safety color. <strong>Stairs, ramps and pool decks</strong> belong to slip-resistant products like Sure Step; a smooth gloss paint on a wet step is a genuine fall hazard, and no amount of good looks is worth that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Long Painted Concrete Lasts, and How to Stretch It
          </h2>

          <p>
            With good prep, a one-part floor paint commonly lasts three to five years in a garage, five to seven in a basement or utility room, and two to four on an exposed patio, where UV and freeze-thaw take their toll. Those ranges are wide because the slab, traffic and climate matter more than the brand. A floor that was etched, dry and cured properly sits at the top of the range; a floor that was painted over a sealer may not make it to the first winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Day-to-day care is simple. Sweep or dust-mop grit regularly, because sand and road grit act like sandpaper underfoot. Mop with plain water or a mild, pH-neutral cleaner, avoiding harsh solvents and strong degreasers that can soften acrylic films. Wipe up oil, brake fluid and gasoline promptly. Put felt pads under furniture legs and a rubber mat where a workbench stool rolls back and forth.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best long-term strategy is to refresh before failure. When the traffic lanes start to look thin but the paint is still bonded, clean the floor, scuff-sand those areas lightly, and roll a single maintenance coat. That costs a gallon or two and an afternoon. Once paint is peeling in sheets, the floor needs stripping or grinding back to bare concrete, which is several times the work.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you find yourself repainting a garage every two years, take that as a sign the space has outgrown paint. A two-part system, or a penetrating sealer that leaves the concrete bare but protected, may cost more up front but less over a decade. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealers</a> covers the bare-concrete route if you would rather never repaint at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over a sealer or curing compound</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many new slabs are finished with a curing compound, and many older floors were sealed at some point. Paint sticks to that layer, not to the concrete, and peels with it. If water beads on the floor, the surface must be stripped or ground before any paint goes down, no matter what the can says about adhesion.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the moisture test on a basement slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water vapor rising through a below-grade slab pushes paint off from beneath, producing blisters and white powdery patches. A day under taped plastic sheeting reveals the problem before you spend money. If droplets appear, solve the moisture source first or choose a product labeled for damp conditions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using interior wall paint on the floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wall paint is formulated to look good on a vertical surface that nobody walks on. On a floor it stays soft, scuffs under shoes, and wears through in weeks. Floor enamels use harder resins for abrasion and cleaning resistance. Saving twenty dollars a gallon on the wrong paint costs the entire job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying it on thick to save a coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy coat seems efficient but stays soft underneath, traps moisture, and is far more likely to peel or wrinkle. Floor paints are designed to build up in two thin coats. Roll it out until the film is even and translucent at the edges, then let it dry before the second coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking on it too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most floor paints are dry to walk on in a day but need about a week, sometimes longer in cool weather, before a car can sit on them. Hot tires soften and grab under-cured paint, then pull it off as they roll away. Park outside for the full vehicle cure listed on the can.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best paint for a concrete floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, an epoxy-modified acrylic floor paint such as BEHR Premium 1-Part Epoxy is the best all-round choice, because it is tougher than plain latex but still rolls on like paint. For patios and living spaces, a premium acrylic enamel looks better. For stairs and wet areas, choose a slip-resistant coating. Daily vehicle parking is better served by a two-part system.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to etch concrete before painting it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Power-troweled garage slabs and smooth basement floors are too dense for paint to grip, and etching opens the surface so the paint can bond. Older, rough or previously ground slabs may already have enough profile. A simple guide is texture: if the floor feels smoother than medium sandpaper, etch or grind it before painting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint concrete that already has paint on it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as the old paint is firmly bonded and compatible. Clean it, scuff-sand it to remove the gloss, and test adhesion with duct tape in several places. If old paint lifts with the tape, scrape or grind those areas back to bare concrete. Avoid putting latex over a glossy oil-based floor enamel without a bonding primer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does concrete floor paint take to dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most one-part floor paints are dry to the touch in one to four hours and ready for a second coat in about four to twelve hours, depending on the product and weather. Light foot traffic is generally fine after a day, furniture after a few days, and vehicles after roughly a week. Cool, humid conditions lengthen every stage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is concrete floor paint slippery when wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Smooth satin and gloss floor paints can be slippery when wet, especially outdoors or on stairs. Add an anti-slip additive to the final coat or choose a textured product such as INSL-X Sure Step or KILZ Over Armor for steps, ramps, pool decks and entries. Indoors, satin finishes are usually safe for dry floors but use mats near doors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does painted concrete last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expect roughly three to five years in a garage with car traffic, five to seven years in a basement or utility space, and two to four years on an exposed patio, assuming good prep. Floors that were properly cleaned, etched and moisture-tested last longest. Rolling a maintenance coat before the paint wears through extends the life considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is paint or epoxy better for a garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A two-part epoxy is more durable, thicker and much better at resisting hot tire pickup, chemicals and abrasion. One-part floor paint is cheaper, easier and more forgiving, and perfectly good for garages used mainly for storage, hobbies or occasional parking. If you park daily and want a floor that lasts many years, a two-part coating is worth the extra cost.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
