          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most concrete floors that see feet rather than tires, a one-part epoxy-modified acrylic like BEHR Premium is the best mix of toughness, ease and cost, with Rust-Oleum's EpoxyShield paint close behind for garages. Use Benjamin Moore Floor and Patio where looks matter, Tread-Plex where boots and carts do, and Sure Step or Over Armor anywhere traction or a rough slab is the problem.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you pick, the floor is decided before the can is opened. Run the water-drop and plastic-sheet tests, degrease and etch, roll two thin coats in mild weather, and wait the full cure before loading the floor. Do that, and a painted slab will look good for years and refresh with a single coat when it starts to wear.
